INT. LAKESIDE BOATHOUSE - NIGHT

Dust and mildew. A canoe on sawhorses. Rain on the tin roof. One camp lantern on an oil drum.

ELLIOTT VANCE, forties, sits on an overturned crate with his hands bound in front of him. His shirt is wet. His eyes are open.

CLEMENTINE "CLEM" ASHWORTH, thirties, stands by the door, a revolver at her hip. Across the boathouse, DR. SAMUEL ABIODUN, sixties, rinses a cloth in a bucket. At the far window, NIAMH QUINLAN, twenties, watches the dock.

ELLIOTT
Clem, this is a mistake.

CLEM
It's not a mistake, Elliott. It's a Tuesday.

SAMUEL
Elliott, the cloth is cold. Hold still.

ELLIOTT
Sam. Sam, you're a doctor.

SAMUEL
I know what I am.

NIAMH
(not turning)
Headlights, Clem. On the north road.

Clem straightens. She crosses to the window and looks, and Niamh steps aside to let her.

CLEM
That's Duncan.

NIAMH
He's early.

CLEM
He's never early.

ELLIOTT
Then he knows something.

CLEM
(quietly)
Shut up, Elliott.

SAMUEL
We should leave.

CLEM
We should finish.

Sam looks at the revolver, at Elliott, at the rain on the glass. He puts down the cloth.

SAMUEL
Clem. I won't.

CLEM
Sam --

SAMUEL
I won't.

Niamh looks from one to the other. The headlights swing across the boathouse wall and fall away.

NIAMH
He's turned around.

A long breath. Clem lowers the revolver an inch. Elliott, shaking, lets out a sound that is half a laugh.

CLEM
Cut him loose.

NIAMH
Are you sure?

CLEM
No.

She does it anyway.
